1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,283
A module is how you add your own code to Orchard Core.

2
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:08,607
In this video, you create a module from the project template, describe it

3
00:00:08,607 --> 00:00:13,860
in its manifest, reference it from your web project, and enable it from the Features page.

4
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:23,906
A module is a .NET class library that Orchard Core loads into your web application.

5
00:00:23,906 --> 00:00:29,720
It can contain services, controllers, views, and static files such as scripts and styles.

6
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:32,778
A manifest describes the module:

7
00:00:32,778 --> 00:00:35,815
its name, its description, and its category.

8
00:00:35,815 --> 00:00:39,520
The Features page shows these when it lists the module.

9
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:43,012
A module provides one or more features.

10
00:00:43,012 --> 00:00:48,555
Each tenant, meaning each site your application hosts, enables the features it needs.

11
00:00:48,555 --> 00:00:52,820
The code of a feature runs only for the tenants that enabled it.

12
00:00:53,570 --> 00:00:57,917
The modules that come with Orchard Core, such as Features, are built the

13
00:00:57,917 --> 00:01:00,815
same way, with a manifest and a Startup class.

14
00:01:00,815 --> 00:01:02,710
Your module works like them.

15
00:01:06,910 --> 00:01:11,650
Install the Orchard Core project templates once, with dotnet new install.

16
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:15,070
The package is called OrchardCore.ProjectTemplates.

17
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:20,480
Then dotnet new creates the module from the CMS module template, and the

18
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,213
dash n option names it.

19
00:01:22,213 --> 00:01:27,650
Following the documentation, the name is the module name followed by dot OrchardCore.

20
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:31,369
Four parts of the generated project matter.

21
00:01:31,369 --> 00:01:36,520
The project file marks it as a module, Manifest.cs describes it, and Startup.cs

22
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:38,943
registers its routes and services.

23
00:01:38,943 --> 00:01:41,670
A controller and a view give it a page.

24
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:47,426
The project file uses the Razor SDK, which precompiles the views of the module.

25
00:01:47,426 --> 00:01:52,549
It references the Orchard Core Module Targets package, and that reference is what

26
00:01:52,549 --> 00:01:55,670
makes the class library an Orchard Core module.

27
00:01:56,420 --> 00:02:00,829
In Startup.cs, the Startup class inherits from StartupBase.

28
00:02:00,829 --> 00:02:06,594
Its Configure method maps the address Home/Index to the Home controller, in the

29
00:02:06,594 --> 00:02:08,900
area named after the module.

30
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:12,918
The Index action of the Home controller returns a view.

31
00:02:12,918 --> 00:02:16,830
Open the Index view in the Views, Home folder, and replace its text

32
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:19,080
with a heading that greets visitors.

33
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,928
The manifest is an assembly attribute in Manifest.cs.

34
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:31,565
Name is the label the Features page shows, so change it to Greetings.

35
00:02:31,565 --> 00:02:34,100
Description appears under the name.

36
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:39,301
Category sets the group the module is listed in, here Samples.

37
00:02:39,301 --> 00:02:44,930
Author, Website, and Version record who made the module and which version it is.

38
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,788
Name changes the label, not the identity.

39
00:02:48,788 --> 00:02:55,400
The feature id stays the name of the assembly, Greetings.OrchardCore, and recipes use that id.

40
00:02:56,150 --> 00:03:00,327
Dependencies lists the ids of the features your module needs.

41
00:03:00,327 --> 00:03:06,250
Enabling the module enables them too, and the Features page shows them beside the module.

42
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,614
A separate video covers writing the same metadata in the project file instead

43
00:03:11,614 --> 00:03:13,170
of in a manifest file.

44
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:21,502
If you do not have a site, the CMS web application template creates

45
00:03:21,502 --> 00:03:23,031
a web project for it.

46
00:03:23,031 --> 00:03:26,710
Its project file references Orchard Core through a package.

47
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:31,697
The web project registers Orchard Core in Program.cs.

48
00:03:31,697 --> 00:03:38,160
AddOrchardCms adds its services, and UseOrchardCore adds it to the request pipeline.

49
00:03:38,910 --> 00:03:41,990
This project must reference the module project.

50
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:47,919
In Visual Studio, right-click Dependencies, choose Add Project Reference, and select the module.

51
00:03:47,919 --> 00:03:51,290
The project file then holds a ProjectReference item.

52
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:56,672
When the web project builds, Orchard Core records the name of each referenced

53
00:03:56,672 --> 00:03:58,291
module in its assembly.

54
00:03:58,291 --> 00:04:03,370
At startup, it loads the recorded modules, and that is why the reference is required.

55
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,642
Run the site with dotnet run, which builds it first.

56
00:04:07,642 --> 00:04:10,860
After any change to the manifest, run it again.

57
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:17,755
With the site running, open the admin.

58
00:04:17,755 --> 00:04:20,818
In the admin menu, open Tools, then Features.

59
00:04:20,818 --> 00:04:25,623
The page lists the features of the installed modules, grouped by category.

60
00:04:26,074 --> 00:04:28,165
Type Greetings in the Search box.

61
00:04:28,165 --> 00:04:32,401
The search matches the name, the feature id, and the description, and the

62
00:04:32,401 --> 00:04:34,064
list narrows as you type.

63
00:04:34,521 --> 00:04:39,278
The module is listed in the Samples group, the category from the manifest.

64
00:04:39,278 --> 00:04:43,975
The row shows the name, the feature id that recipes use, the description,

65
00:04:43,975 --> 00:04:45,581
and a Disabled badge.

66
00:04:46,043 --> 00:04:47,243
Select Enable.

67
00:04:47,243 --> 00:04:51,563
The page reloads and shows a message that the feature was enabled.

68
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:53,942
Search for Greetings again.

69
00:04:53,942 --> 00:04:57,720
The row shows an Enabled badge, and its button reads Disable.

70
00:04:58,186 --> 00:05:01,566
Enabling a feature changes the current tenant only.

71
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:06,486
Another tenant of the same application keeps its own set of enabled features.

72
00:05:10,774 --> 00:05:15,194
With the feature enabled, the Startup class of the module runs for this

73
00:05:15,194 --> 00:05:17,376
tenant, and its route is active.

74
00:05:17,376 --> 00:05:18,984
Let's open Home/Index.

75
00:05:19,447 --> 00:05:22,752
The page shows the heading from the view of the module, inside the

76
00:05:22,752 --> 00:05:24,267
layout of the site's theme.

77
00:05:28,467 --> 00:05:33,372
To enable the module automatically, add a Feature step to a recipe, such

78
00:05:33,372 --> 00:05:35,762
as a setup or deployment recipe.

79
00:05:35,762 --> 00:05:40,101
Its enable list holds feature ids, and the id of this module is

80
00:05:40,101 --> 00:05:43,057
its assembly name, Greetings.OrchardCore.

81
00:05:47,257 --> 00:05:51,467
If the module does not appear on the Features page, check two things.

82
00:05:51,467 --> 00:05:56,408
The module project must reference the Orchard Core Module Targets package, and the

83
00:05:56,408 --> 00:05:59,327
web project must reference the module project.

84
00:06:00,077 --> 00:06:01,645
Then run the site again.

85
00:06:01,645 --> 00:06:06,036
Orchard Core records the referenced modules when the web project builds, so an

86
00:06:06,036 --> 00:06:09,747
added reference or a changed manifest takes effect after a build.

87
00:06:11,747 --> 00:06:17,068
A module is a project that references the Module Targets package, describes itself

88
00:06:17,068 --> 00:06:20,515
in a manifest, and is enabled from Tools, Features.

89
00:06:20,515 --> 00:06:22,087
Thanks for watching.

